{
  "dom_id": "step-e12745",
  "name": "Example: Negating Existential Statements",
  "problem": "Negate the following statement: \n\n$\\qquad$ Some quadrilaterals are squares.",
  "worked_solution": "Let $Q$ be the set of all quadrilaterals and $S$ the set of all squares. The given statement can be re-phrased as follows:\n\n$\\qquad$ There exists $q \\in Q$ such that $q \\in S$\n\nIntroducing the existential quantifier, we get the following:\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\exists q \\in Q, \\, q \\in S.$\n\nNegating this, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u00ac(\u2203q\\in Q,\\,q\\in S) & \u2261\u2200q\\in Q,\\,\u00ac(q\\in S) \\\\  & \u2261\u2200q\\in Q,\\,q\\notin S\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich reads \n\n$\\qquad$ For all $q \\in Q,$ $q \\not\\in S.$\n\nor simply\n\n$\\qquad$ All quadrilaterals are not squares.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
